---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Narge Specification">
  <h1>Narge Specification</h1>

  <section class="intro">
    <p class="lead">
      Narge is a class of visual content. It is not a brand, a product, a filter, a religion, or a law.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Version 0.2.</strong> 27 August 2026.
    </p>
    <p>
      The spoken word is <strong>NARJ</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Definition</h2>
    <p>
      A file, stream, or post is <strong>Narge</strong> when it is a video or still (including photorealistic AI) 
      of one or more real or photorealistic humans, produced or posted <strong>primarily to sexually arouse</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      That includes sex acts, masturbation, nudes, and partial nudes when that is the purpose. 
      If the purpose is not arousal, it is not Narge.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the payload is already a crime under CSAM law, this class does not apply.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Not Narge</h2>
    <p>
      Anything that is already a CSAM crime. Written-only text. Non-photoreal illustration. Medical, sex education, 
      news, documentary, breastfeeding, a clothing sale, or a story film, when the purpose is not arousal.
    </p>
    <p>
      A clip or still cut from a story to arouse is Narge. A link inherits the class of the destination payload.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Rules</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Look at the payload, not the domain.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Purpose decides.</strong> Two providers may disagree at the margin of a specific picture. 
          That is expected. There are no numbered ratings and no extra classes.</li>
      <li><strong>Photoreal counts.</strong> Photoreal AI or a deepfake is the same payload if it meets this definition. 
          Consent is a different stack.</li>
      <li><strong>The bar is malware, not prohibition.</strong> The class can sit off the ordinary trusted path 
          and still be findable if someone tries.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How to Use the Word</h2>
    <p>
      That's Narge. A Narge site. This site may contain Narge. This site is promoting Narge. Blocks Narge. A Narge warning.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Do not number it.</strong> There is no Narge-1. The bare word means the definition above.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Host List Process</h2>
    <p>
      The open process for producing a hostname list based on this definition is documented separately. 
      See the <a href="https://github.com/dov-max/narge-spec/blob/main/LIST.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LIST.md</a> file 
      in the repository.
    </p>

    <h3>Temporary Feed</h3>
    <p>
      Until a public host registry exists, the list pulls from the 
      <a href="https://github.com/StevenBlack/hosts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">StevenBlack hosts list</a> 
      (MIT license, attributed). This is an approximation of Narge, not Narge. It is pulled on a timer.
    </p>

    <h3>Overlay Files</h3>
    <p>
      Two small public files add corrections:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>overlay/block.txt</code> — extra hosts to block</li>
      <li><code>overlay/allow.txt</code> — StevenBlack entries to unblock</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These files exist to refine the temporary feed until a later public host registry exists.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Resolver Performance Metrics</h2>
    <p>
      Cutline DNS resolvers publish standardized latency metrics to measure resolver performance.
    </p>

    <h3>Latency Definition</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Resolver Latency</strong> measures the time from when a DNS query is received by the resolver 
      to when the answer is sent back. This is the resolver's processing time only.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What is included:</strong> Query parsing, blocklist lookup, upstream resolution (if not blocked), 
      response assembly, and transmission.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What is NOT included:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Last-mile network latency between the client and resolver</li>
      <li>Client operating system DNS delays</li>
      <li>Browser Secure DNS (DoH) connection overhead</li>
      <li>Client-side caching</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Published Metrics</h3>
    <p>
      Cutline publishes two latency percentiles, updated continuously:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>p50 (median):</strong> The middle value — 50% of queries are faster, 50% are slower</li>
      <li><strong>p95 (95th percentile):</strong> 95% of queries complete faster than this value</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Measurement window:</strong> Last 24 hours<br>
      <strong>Granularity:</strong> Per resolver site (EWR and LAX reported separately)<br>
      <strong>Units:</strong> Milliseconds (ms)
    </p>
    <p>
      See live metrics on the <a href="/stats">Stats page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Copyright</h2>
    <p>
      This document is dedicated to the public domain under 
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0 1.0</a>. 
      Anyone may copy, change, or sell the text. There is no licensor.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Class Name</h2>
    <p>
      Narge, as used in this document, is a classification token. It is not a product name. 
      The author claims no trademark in that use and asks others not to treat it as a brand.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="links">
    <h2>Full Specification</h2>
    <p>
      The complete, authoritative specification and list process are maintained in the GitHub repository:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/dov-max/narge-spec/blob/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">README.md</a> — Narge definition</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/dov-max/narge-spec/blob/main/LIST.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LIST.md</a> — Host list process</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/dov-max/narge-spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dov-max/narge-spec</a> — Full repository</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Layout>

